WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:09.860
Dynamic code generation includes reflection emit, compiled expressions, runtime proxies, generated accessors, and other code-like runtime behavior.

2
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:17.160
These techniques can be legitimate in serializers, ORMs, proxy frameworks, test tools, and performance-sensitive libraries.

3
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.840
Generated behavior should be traceable to trusted code and trusted configuration.

4
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:29.620
A generated accessor, proxy, or expression may not appear directly in source, but the rule that produced it should be understandable.

5
00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:36.120
Reviewers should be able to answer what is generated, from which inputs, and under whose control.

6
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.240
Avoid generating executable behavior from user-controlled input.

7
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:49.860
If a dynamic generator accepts configuration, templates, expressions, or metadata, define which sources are trusted and which values are only data.

8
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.440
A dynamic mechanism should not turn untrusted text into runtime behavior.

9
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.080
Dynamic code can reduce reviewability because the final behavior may not appear directly in source.

10
00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.940
Logging and diagnostics should make generated behavior understandable without exposing sensitive data.

11
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.640
Test coverage should exercise generated paths, not only the static code around them.

12
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:19.200
Runtime generation may be limited or unsupported in some deployment models, restricted environments, or ahead-of-time scenarios.

13
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:25.220
Deployment targets should be part of the design review so a dynamic feature does not become a production reliability issue.
